  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Multiple Homes**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how to handle the possibility of a user managing multiple homes, as implied by the Italian question. The initial design centered on meal planning and management within a single home. I\'m reassessing to ensure the user can seamlessly handle multiple residences, avoiding any user experience limitations.\n\n\n**Expanding Home Management**\n\nI\'m now thinking specifically about how the app should handle multiple homes. The core concept is that users need to define different \"spaces,\" like \"Daniele\'s Home\" and \"Partner\'s Home.\" These spaces can then be selected to filter the weekly menu and grocery lists. This means a user could quickly assign meals to a specific house or designate where items should be purchased. The app must ensure easy switching between these spaces for efficient spesa and menu planning.\n\n\n**Designing Home Spaces**\n\nI\'m now integrating an effortless \"Casa Switcher\" to manage multiple households. The core will be a header displaying the active home, which when clicked, unveils a dropdown of saved homes. Users can instantly switch between homes, add new ones with a 6-digit code or creation, or manage existing ones. I\'m also ensuring each meal can optionally be assigned to a specific home or \"other\" location.\n\n\n**Implementing Home Location**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the code updates required by location-specific shopping lists. I\'ve found the `HouseholdContext` currently only handles a single household code, which is something I plan to adjust. I\'m expanding it to include an array of `savedHouseholds` in `localStorage`, and I\'ll add methods for switching and removing houses. Moreover, I\'m updating `MealSlot` and `ShoppingItem` to include optional location properties and defining the navigation, and how the user interface will reflect this new functionality.\n\n\n"
