  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Association Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the term \"together/separate\" should change based on the number of people associated with a residence. It seems that a simple one-to-one relationship is in order here, where the variable can take on unique states based on the size of the set. My intention is to come up with a formula.\n\n\n**Elaborating Group Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now detailing how group dynamics shift with various household sizes. For a single occupant, the \"together/separate\" concept doesn\'t apply. For two people, it\'s a simple binary choice. However, with larger groups, the possibilities become combinatorial. Three could eat alone or two together, while four opens up combinations: everyone solo, pairs, trios and a solo, etc. I\'m exploring representing these relationships mathematically.\n\n\n**Clarifying Meal Grouping**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the meal grouping logic. For a single person, there\'s no \"together/separate\" toggle, just a meal slot. For two people, it\'s a simple binary toggle: \"Together\" or \"Separate.\" However, for three or more, it becomes more complex, allowing \"All Together\", \"All Separate\", or custom subgroups. I\'m exploring how best to represent these dynamic meal arrangements for diverse group sizes.\n\n\n**Visualizing Meal Groups**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the visual interface design for meal groups. Each meal will display its \"Table Groups\" (`groups: MealGroup[]`). Each `MealGroup` includes `memberIds: string[]`, `dishes: Dish[]`, and optional `notes?` I\'m considering intuitive buttons or menus for quickly changing combinations such as \"All Together\", \"Everyone Separate\", or \"Create/Modify Groups\" to interactively move people between groups.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Data Migration**\n\nI\'m now implementing retrocompatibility for older household data. It\'s time to check the files to see where data structures need updating. I\'m focusing on converting old `users` fields, which contained `daniele` and `partner`, into a dynamic `members` array, keeping the `users` property for fallback. In `MealSlot`, I\'m replacing individual dish fields with a `groups` array of `MealGroup` objects for more flexibility. There\'s a helper function planned to normalize old meal slots into the new `groups` structure during data migration.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_371416"
      name: "view_file"
      arguments_json: "{\"AbsolutePath\":\"/src/types.ts\", \"ContentOffset\":0, \"EndLine\":115, \"StartLine\":1, \"toolAction\":\"Viewing types.ts\", \"toolSummary\":\"View types.ts\"}"
      thought_signature: ""
      original_name: "view_file"
