    thinking: "**Analyzing User Requests**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the user\'s requests. Their questions touch upon user switching (partner vs. Daniele), multiple \"cases,\" and scaling based on the number of people. I\'m focusing on the implied functionalities and their potential interactions. I intend to build the structure based on this analysis.\n\n\n**Clarifying House Association**\n\nI\'m now clarifying how \"persone associate a quella casa\" are configured. Each household can have one or more associated individuals. I\'m focusing on user interface design to ensure it\'s easy to configure the association and easy to display in the header or in a dedicated modal. My goal is to support cases with one, two, or multiple people with clarity.\n\n\n**Refining Association Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the logic for managing household associations. Handling single individuals is now straightforward: no \"Together/Separate\" toggle is needed, and meal slots show directly for the person. For two people, a clear toggle is implemented. When multiple people are involved (3+), I\'m focused on enabling customizable sub-grouping configurations, as this offers flexible mealtime customization.\n\n\n**Defining Grouping Options**\n\nI\'m now detailing the flexible grouping options needed. Considering various scenarios (3 or 4 people), I\'ve expanded on examples. I\'m focusing on UI elements to present and modify group configurations in a simple, friendly, and very direct way to users. I also need to provide preset choices. The display of the current arrangement and easy access to editing with a button will be very clear and interactive.\n\n\n**Revising User Switching**\n\nI\'m now revising the user switching design. Single users render no switcher. For multiple people, a more intuitive \"Who\'s using the app?\" indicator is better, not a generic \"switch user.\" This clarifies who is marking groceries (\"Spuntato da Daniele\") or viewing \"i miei piatti.\" Meal slots will clearly indicate the group and member names.\n\n\n"
