  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Login Logic**\n\nI\'ve been drilling down on the user login logic. I realized that when a user wasn\'t logged in, the `currentUserName` was defaulting to `\'Daniele\'`! So, anyone who checked something off without logging in was being falsely attributed to Daniele! I\'ve updated the code so that if a user is *not* logged in, the person checking it off is just \"someone\".\n\n\n**Updating User Handling**\n\nI\'ve been working on improving user handling. Now, when a user isn\'t logged in, they\'re correctly identified as \"Guest\" with a `\360\237\221\244` avatar. I\'ve updated the `Navigation.tsx` component to handle both logged-in Google users, using their `photoURL` or `displayName` if available, and fallback names and avatars. The code is much more robust now!\n\n\n**Improving User Display**\n\nI\'ve significantly refined the user display in the navigation. The component now shows the Google user\'s `photoURL` if available. If not, the code will show the first letter of their `displayName` or default to \"G\". The display has improved, showing a clearer representation for logged-in users. The component now renders Google users\' avatars.\n\n\n**Enhancing Login Display**\n\nI\'m now focusing on further enhancing the login display. I\'ve updated the component to handle the situation where a user might not have a full name, and I\'ve ensured a fallback to \"Account\" if there is no display name from google. The logout button now uses a more explicit title for better user experience. I\'ve improved the overall aesthetic and user experience of this feature.\n\n\n**Analyzing Login State**\n\nI\'m now thoroughly examining how the login state is displayed. When a user is *not* logged in, the application correctly presents the \"Accedi con Google\" button. I\'m focusing on the component\'s conditional rendering based on user login status. The button text changes as appropriate. The `currentUserAvatar` and `currentUserName` elements are properly integrated, and the logic is streamlined.\n\n\n**Clarifying Guest Access**\n\nI\'m now clarifying guest access. Without login, the avatar is definitively `\360\237\221\244`, with the label \"Guest\" (or \"Ospite\"). Clicking it will take them to Settings, or suggest a Google login or persona selection. I am making sure the state and presentation are clear. When logged in, Google data will be prioritized for avatar and name.\n\n\n**Refining Guest Display**\n\nI\'m now refining the guest profile display. I\'ve updated the component to include a clear \"Guest Profile\" label (or \"Profilo Ospite\") and a \"Not Authenticated\" badge. I\'ve added a helpful message explaining the benefits of logging in with Google. The design clearly distinguishes guest access, prompting users to log in for full functionality.\n\n\n**Investigating Guest Settings**\n\nI\'m now investigating guest settings. I am making sure that an unauthenticated user can still see the `\360\237\221\244 Guest` display, as well as have the option to log in. I\'m checking how the `currentRole` in `HouseholdContext.tsx` can be \'guest\' when a user is not logged in. I am focusing on what an unauthenticated user can do, like switching between Daniele and Partner to view their dishes. I need to make sure that guests can see their own data.\n\n\n"
