    thinking: "**Analyzing Navigation Behavior**\n\nI\'ve been examining the `Navigation.tsx` file, specifically the header logic. I\'m focusing on the condition where `!user` is true. Specifically, I am scrutinizing line 262 to understand how the login with Google button works in that context. This is about to resolve some open questions!\n\n\n**Examining Component Styling**\n\nI\'m now diving into the styling details within `Navigation.tsx`, especially the conditional rendering. I\'m focusing on the login button\'s class names and their impact on the visual presentation. I want to understand how the responsive design works with the `sm:` prefixes, and how the button behaves on smaller screens. The use of Tailwind classes is becoming clearer, and I\'m interested in how that styling influences user experience.\n\n\n**Clarifying Conditional Rendering**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the conditional rendering for the profile indication badge when the user is not authenticated. It\'s becoming apparent that `currentUserAvatar` defaults to `\'\360\237\221\244\'` and `currentUserName` to `\'Guest\'`. I\'m closely examining the button\'s class names and the `hidden sm:inline` directives, and understanding how different screen sizes interact. This should help to clear up the differences between the login and guest states.\n\n\n**Investigating User Authentication States**\n\nI\'m now comparing the logged-in user state, with a Google account, to the case of a user that has no associated account. The previous code snippet is crucial: it displays user details (profile picture or fallback icon) and name. If the user has a Google account, I need to check how the UI differentiates the Google login button from the user\'s information. I\'m focusing on the condition where `user` is present, especially the two buttons shown in that case. I need to understand this to differentiate the authenticated states.\n\n\n**Resolving Guest Display**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the issue: when unauthenticated, the avatar incorrectly displayed \"\360\237\221\250\342\200\215\360\237\215\263 Daniele\" instead of \"\360\237\221\244 Guest,\" as the user observed. I am now examining `HouseholdContext.tsx`, particularly how the `isGuest`, `currentUserName` and `currentUserAvatar` are computed. I need to ensure the correct fallback is consistently used to prevent this misrepresentation. I\'m also checking how shopping list items are toggled to confirm that user identity is handled appropriately there, too.\n\n\n"
