    thinking: "**Analyzing Navigation Component**\n\nI\'m currently focused on pinpointing the rendering locations within `src/components/Navigation.tsx` for the user\'s avatar, name, and profile badge. The specific code snippet for pending shopping list items is under review as well. I want to build a better understanding of how the navigation component works to improve it.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Display Elements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the specific lines responsible for rendering the user\'s name, avatar, and other profile details, focusing on the conditional rendering within `Navigation.tsx`. The code that handles the display of a guest user versus an authenticated user is of particular interest. I\'m noting the use of fallback values and how the avatar or name might display based on different conditions.\n\n\n**Examining User Profile Data**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the conditional rendering within `Navigation.tsx` for user profile data, particularly the avatar and display name. I\'m analyzing the logic that decides whether to display a full image or a user\'s initials, and how that relates to the fallback values. I\'m also examining the display name truncation for larger screens.\n\n\n**Investigating Google Sign-In**\n\nI\'m now diving into the Google Sign-In button component within the `Navigation.tsx` file. I am looking at its styling and functionality to understand how it integrates with user authentication, focusing on the conditional rendering and the SVG icon used. I need to understand its role in linking to the Google authentication flow.\n\n\n\n**Exploring Authentication Button**\n\nI am now closely examining the conditional rendering logic related to the Google sign-in button within `Navigation.tsx`. I am analyzing how the SVG icon is implemented and its styling. Also, I am now focused on the differences in the button\'s display on smaller and larger screens (mobile vs desktop). I\'m looking at how the \"Accedi\" (Login) text is handled, based on screen size, to ensure user experience.\n\n\n**Consolidating Navigation Elements**\n\nI\'m now considering the navigation elements\' behavior when a user *is* logged in. I\'m seeing a possible redundancy: both a Google button and a profile indicator. I am thinking of a combined display showing the Google account info on the left, and a combined `\360\237\221\244 Guest` display, if not logged in. My current goal is an elegant consolidation for a cleaner user interface. I\'m focusing on the component\'s conditional rendering to refine this logic and improve the user experience.\n\n\n**Refining Guest Display**\n\nI\'m now refining the guest display logic. When not logged in, the Navigation component will feature \"Accedi con Google\" and \"\360\237\221\244 Guest.\" If logged in, the Google account chip appears alongside the user\'s role. I\'ve turned my focus now to `SettingsView.tsx` and have been analyzing lines 190-248 to refine the guest experience there, ensuring it\'s prominent and friendly, including \"Profilo Guest (Ospite)\" and the \"Accesso non effettuato\" badge, along with a clear call to action to log in via Google. I am also looking at the section \"Chi usa questo smartphone adesso\" and lines 654-710 to ensure a clear experience there too.\n\n\n**Adjusting Guest Role**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the default guest role within `HouseholdContext.tsx` and have found an important detail on lines 147-148: the original implementation defaulted to \'daniele\' if a role wasn\'t set. I plan to change this and prioritize \'guest\' to refine the experience. I\'m focusing on the code now to correct this potential user experience issue.\n\n\n**Clarifying Guest Logic**\n\nI am now clarifying the guest logic in `HouseholdContext.tsx`. When there\'s no user, `currentRole` defaults to \"guest,\" ensuring a consistent and expected behavior. I\'m focusing on lines 960 to understand how names are rendered. When user details are unavailable, \"Guest\" is used.\n\n\n"
